// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// VP-ADJUNCT PREPOSITIONS REQUIRE A VERBAL FINITE VP (fin_any -> fin). Shadowing only.
//
// Narrower than vpadj-bind-finiteness.esl, which bound the feature across all 11 and cost the
// «MSI is associated with …» pin while RAISING skeletons 179 -> 207 (binding un-collapses what the
// wildcard was masking). This changes the FEATURE rather than introducing a binder.
//
// DEFECT TARGETED: `fin_any` meets ANYTHING, including `adj`. So a VP-adjunct PP may attach to an
// ADJECTIVAL predication — which is exactly how «We ascertained MSI status with sequencing» reaches
// the root as a copula-less predicate nominal, `is_a(speaker, Σ…ascertained MSI status…)`, with the
// verb absorbed into the nominal and no `ascertain` relation anywhere (probe d532cd3 / 3ae672d).
// Requiring `fin` excludes `adj` while leaving genuinely verbal attachment intact.
//
// Does NOT touch the argument-marker `with` (`cat_pp_arg(prep_with)/NP`) — that entry has no
// `cat_s` at all, so the «MSI is associated with notable responses to …» pin, which needs `with`
// ABSORBED as an argument, is untouched by construction.
//
// KILL CRITERION: grammar-gap 0 and expected-hits 60/62.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RESULT 2026-08-01: the FIRST skeleton reduction of the programme, at the cost of 3 pins.
//
//   grammar-gap      0  -> 0     held
//   encoded          12 -> 13    higher (better resolution)
//   total-readings   988 -> 790  -198
//   total-skeletons  179 -> 160  -19   <- first reduction
//   expected-hits    60 -> 57
//
// Both predictions held: skeletons FELL rather than rose (confirming the `cat_fin_forall` binder,
// not the restriction, is what un-collapsed derivations to 207), and the mechanism is real.
//
// The 3 lost pins share ONE cause — they are legitimately NON-FINITE but VERBAL:
//   «MSI CAN arise from Lynch syndrome.»                        modal => VP is `bse`
//   «… WOULD give rise to synthetic-lethal relationships.»      modal => `bse`
//   «MSI IS ASSOCIATED with notable responses to …»             participial/copular
//
// So `fin` is too narrow in the other direction. The restriction that is actually wanted is ANY
// VERBAL form — fin | bse | inf | ger | pss | pass — but NOT `adj`. No such feature value exists:
// `fin_any` is a wildcard that meets `adj` too, which is precisely the hole this probe exploits.
//
// NEXT: add a `fin_verbal` wildcard to `lexicon:Fin` that meets every verbal form and excludes
// `adj`, and give the VP-adjunct prepositions that instead of `fin_any`. That is a BOOTSTRAP
// ontology change (a new `Fin` ctor), so unlike every probe in this directory it needs a reseed —
// but it is the first change with measured evidence that it should pay for one: -19 skeletons and
// -198 readings, with the only cost traced to a feature distinction the lattice cannot yet express.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

namespace lexicon   = "urn:eigenius:lexicon";
namespace logic     = "urn:eigenius:logic";
namespace ontology  = "urn:eigenius:ontology";
namespace epistemic = "urn:eigenius:reflection:epistemic";
namespace measurements = "urn:eigenius:measurements";
namespace core         = "urn:eigenius:core";

resource lexicon:between_prep : lexicon:LexicalEntry {
    core:description = "grammatical: preposition marking a relation between two things.";
    lexicon:form     = "between";
    lexicon:cat      = type_expr( lexicon:fwd(lexicon:m_all, lexicon:bwd(lexicon:m_all, lexicon:bwd(lexicon:m_all, lexicon:cat_s(lexicon:dcl, lexicon:fin), lexicon:cat_np(lexicon:Entity, lexicon:num_any)), lexicon:bwd(lexicon:m_all, lexicon:cat_s(lexicon:dcl, lexicon:fin), lexicon:cat_np(lexicon:Entity, lexicon:num_any))), lexicon:cat_np(lexicon:Entity, lexicon:num_any)) );
    lexicon:sem      = lexicon:prep_between_sem;
    lexicon:sem_type = type_expr( lexicon:Entity -> (lexicon:Entity -> Prop) -> (lexicon:Entity -> Prop) );
    lexicon:sense    = "between";
    lexicon:grade    = epistemic:declared;
}
resource lexicon:within_prep : lexicon:LexicalEntry {
